BT /F1 10 Tf 14 TL 56 740 Td (Cover Crops in Maize Rotations: Results of a Three-Season Field Study) Tj T* () Tj T* (Summary. We studied whether sowing a legume cover crop between maize seasons improves) Tj T* (yields and soil quality on smallholder farms. Over three seasons, maize grown after a) Tj T* (cover crop yielded more, needed less mineral fertiliser and had fewer weeds than maize) Tj T* (grown after bare fallow. The extra labour for sowing and incorporating the cover crop was) Tj T* (the main drawback reported by farmers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Design. The study ran on twenty-four plots of 0.25 hectares on six farms in the same) Tj T* (district, with four plots per farm. On each farm, two plots received a cover crop of) Tj T* (velvet bean after the maize harvest and two plots were left as bare fallow, the usual) Tj T* (practice in the area. Plots were assigned at random. Maize variety, planting dates and) Tj T* (pest control followed the farmers' usual practice and were the same for all plots on a) Tj T* (farm. Soil samples were taken before the first season and after the third.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Sowing and management. The cover crop was sown by hand within ten days after the harvest,) Tj T* (while the soil still held enough moisture for the seeds to germinate. Sowing later than) Tj T* (that led to poor establishment in two farms in the first season, which is why the window) Tj T* (was kept strict afterwards. The cover crop was cut and left on the surface as mulch three) Tj T* (weeks before maize planting, then worked lightly into the topsoil.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Yields. Averaged over the three seasons, maize yields rose by nine percent on plots with a) Tj T* (cover crop compared with bare fallow plots. The difference was small in the first season) Tj T* (and largest in the third, which suggests that the benefit builds up as soil organic matter) Tj T* (increases. In the dry second season, cover crop plots also lost fewer plants to drought) Tj T* (stress, probably because the mulch kept the soil moist for longer.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Fertiliser. Because velvet bean fixes nitrogen from the air, farmers on cover crop plots) Tj T* (reduced nitrogen fertiliser by a quarter from the second season onwards without losing) Tj T* (yield. This saving covered most of the cost of the cover crop seed. Phosphorus and) Tj T* (potassium applications were not changed.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Soil. Soil organic carbon on cover crop plots increased by 0.3 percentage points over the) Tj T* (three seasons, while it stayed level on bare fallow plots. Infiltration measured with a) Tj T* (simple ring test was faster on cover crop plots, and farmers reported that the soil was) Tj T* (easier to work.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Weeds. Weed biomass at the start of the maize season was forty percent lower on cover crop) Tj T* (plots, because the cover crop and its mulch shaded out weeds during the dry period.) Tj T* (Farmers needed one weeding round less per season on these plots.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Costs and labour. Sowing, cutting and incorporating the cover crop took about six extra) Tj T* (hours of work per hectare each season. Several farmers said that this work falls at a time) Tj T* (of year when labour is scarce, and three of the six farms said they would only continue if) Tj T* (the seed were available locally.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Conclusions. A legume cover crop between maize seasons is worth recommending where seed is) Tj T* (available and labour can be found shortly after the harvest. Extension services should) Tj ET
